WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.679 --> 00:00:04.630 align:start position:0%
 
covering one  square mile of new york city brooklyn's  greenwood cemetery is a national  historic landmark  more than 500 000 people are interred  here including the inventor  samuel morse composer leonard bernstein  artist jean-michel basquiat and rapper  pop smoke  it's also the final resting place for an  untold number of american war veterans  now the cemetery is enlisting an army of  volunteers to help tell the stories of  those  who served throughout our nation's  history  greenwood cemetery's sprawling grounds  over 476  acres in brooklyn new york has been  called a green  oasis a place that serves as solace and  peace for some  historical record for others from its  skyline views of manhattan  to its landmark chapel jeff richmond is  the cemetery's historian it's a constant  treasure hunt here i don't think a week  goes by  where i don't find out about somebody  fascinating  who's interred here at the cemetery  richmond isn't just talking about the  rich and famous  in 2002 he made it his mission to  identify the cemetery's  military veterans starting with the  civil war  at the time did you know how many civil  war  soldiers veterans were buried here i had  no idea  we did have one volunteer and he had  been walking the grounds for 20 years  and he had a list of 250  and so i was young and foolish back then  and thought that if he had 250 perhaps  we would find another 250 and we have  well past 5 000 and it goes beyond just  the name on the headstone  with each veteran tied to an online  biography  after the civil war identifications the  greenwood project turned to world war  one  how did you go about finding out more  about these people well fortunately with  the internet now  with a number of websites there's just  extraordinary information  out there it might be an obituary from  1903  saying here's this person's story and he  served  in and so those are kind of the magic  words  served in the 51st new york infantry and  then we can go from there  the liberation of every town like this  one strasbourg capital of alsace  lorraine  requires staggering supplies of  ammunition world war ii  is greenwood cemetery's current focus  these are people who were there at  normandy  and they landed who fought their way  across the pacific  also we have come across a rosie the  riveter  who uh went to work and so just this  loving description from her daughter  about my mother would dress up for the  factory where she was working  and so she would wear this kind of  industrial outfit but  the daughter was very careful to point  out that  after work she would get back into her  dress  as a lady and come home to her family  family sharing stories and even  documenting them  my grandfather was in the army and my  grandmother was in the navy as a nurse  um and they met while in the army and in  the navy serving  and that's you know history is made  catherine amore is one of about 20  volunteers currently compiling stories  and pictures of the cemetery's world war  ii veterans  including her grandparents sebastian and  susan amore  her grandfather served as a corporal in  the army's seventh armored division  which saw heavy combat in europe from  1944  until the end of the war her grandmother  served in a women's naval reserve unit  known  as the waves waves learned combat  tactics  okay miss third class yeoman we know  when we're licked  they met in louisiana where the seventh  was training and were married  for 41 years after the war i'm learning  more about how much history is out there  of them  whether it's pictures whether it's just  stories that are documented in  newspapers  yeah because i know their lives i knew  what they did but not necessarily those  pieces of history  learning about another generation often  teaches us something have you learned  something from this the greatest  generation  that's a good way to put it right um i  think what i've realized is that every  generation has their struggles and every  generation  has their times that they look back on  and whether they regret it or not  you know saying this is what i can learn  from i can learn from this  and if we don't document it right how do  we learn from it  volunteer barbara buchholz has been  working on the greenwood veterans  project from its inception  she is also the daughter of a world war  ii veteran  as you've learned about some of the  people buried here  it's also made you curious about your  own  father as a veteran my father his uncles  they never talked about  world war ii and we never asked about it  even when we were older and then  unfortunately when our parents passed we  find the pictures  my dad was in normandy he was in belgium  he was in  the european theater italy and  it's a new part of my father that i  didn't know about i'm learning about my  father  and i didn't give much thought to world  war ii  until recently for you what stood out  in learning these individual stories  just how important it is  us for us to remember them and tell  their story and to focus on  just what makes life worth living  volunteer jessica waverka doesn't have a  familial tie to any of greenwood's  veterans  i want to know what motivates them what  motivates them to come from a small  midwestern town to new york they joined  world war  ii what inspired them to enlist but  you'd never know it when hearing her  talk  about those she's researched like  private frederick budgel  he was 17 years old when he's enlisted a  few months shy of 18.  he went down there on a thursday on a  tuesday he was sworn in  went off to world war ii came back he  had nine children  he was an iron worker proud iron worker  for 45 years  we were with her when she visited  private budgel's gravesite for the first  time  what does it mean to you to see the  headstone this is no longer a stranger  this is someone  i have to think you feel like you know  it makes me proud to be part of his  story  and to part of telling his story it's a  pride greenwood's other volunteers and  jeff richmond share  by sharing the stories of those who  sacrificed  to serve our country why for you  is this so important ultimately a  cemetery is about  remembrance and so these monuments and  these memorials  that are out there remember the people  but there are stories  behind each of these lives  and so we're trying to share those  stories and collect  those stories and it's just been a  wonderful opportunity and it's stories  that help us  learn history learn from history there  was one volunteer barbara who we spoke  with  and she knew her dad was in world war ii  but she never asked any questions and  unfortunately he's gone but now she's  started  looking into that and wondering about it  so nice to see the mom  take her kids there so they can learn  about history and learn about why people  made that choice  to serve yeah i think about it it just  makes  your relatives those people who came  before us  real like they were here they made  contributions  and they're in many ways like us yeah we  don't think of them that way though but  that  that is exactly it it's that remembrance  and they are still looking  for more veterans that are at greenwood  cemetery if you have information  on a world war ii veteran who is buried  at greenwood you can fill out an  information form online at  greendashwood.com  and yes they do expect maybe to turn  this into projects for also like the  korean war and other wars that came past

